The 
\series bold
Texture
\series default
 group displays an image as a textured plane: the active ImageJ image (static, load again after changes, e.g., of the LUT), the active ImageJ image (dynamic, the texture is updated with the image) or an image file. 
\series bold
Flip Vertically
\series default
 flips the texture, height map, volume and iso surface (by default the image is displayed like in ImageJ with 0,0 at the top left).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
Height Map
\series default
 group displays the pixel values of the active ImageJ image (8-bit, 16-bit, 32-bit, e.g., a digital elevation model or a measurement as a 32-bit image) as the heights of a surface which is textured with the selected texture. 
\series bold
Plot View
\series default
 displays the height map as a surface plot with the axes, the box and the grids of the plot API. 
\series bold
Show Triangles
\series default
 draws the triangles, 
\series bold
Lighted
\series default
 enables the lighting, 
\series bold
Dynamic Heights
\series default
 updates the heights directly from the active image and 
\series bold
Negative Heights (Geo)
\series default
 scales the heights negative for a geographic view. The 
\series bold
Level of Detail
\series default
 (the distance of the vertices in pixels) and the 
\series bold
Height Factor
\series default
 (logarithmic) can be adjusted. 
\series bold
Fit Height
\series default
 sets the height factor so that the range of the heights is half of the longer side of the image.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With 
\series bold
Export Height Map...
\series default
 the height map is exported as a 3D mesh with the colors of the image (LUT): glTF binary (*.glb, with the texture, e.g., for Blender), OBJ (with the texture as *.png), PLY (with vertex colors), STL (3D printing) or VTK legacy (*.vtk, e.g., for ParaView). With the option Export the Image as Texture (group Export, default on) the texture is embedded in the *.glb file or written as a *.png file next to the *.obj file; the other formats get the colors as vertex colors. The z values are the heights multiplied with the height factor. Texture Workaround (Gc) is an option for graphics cards which need another assignment of the textures.
\end_layout

